/**

@page tutorial_new_db Creating a New Collection

@tableofcontents

This tutorial guides you step by step through all functions of
Colman -- from creating the first database to everyday lending and
returning. Each section builds on the previous one, so that by the
end you can run a fully set up school library.

@note Colman currently runs on Linux; the mobile client runs on
Android devices. Windows and macOS are not supported yet.

@note Colman is a Qt application. All button labels in this
tutorial refer to the English user interface. The program uses the
language of your operating system; if it is set to German, the
German tutorial applies.

When Colman starts for the first time, the <b>start page</b>
appears. To set up a new library, click <b>create new
collection</b>.

@section tutorial_new_db_start Start Page

At the top, the start page shows the <b>Colman logo</b>, the title
<b>ColMan</b> with the version number and the subtitle <b>the
collection manager</b>. Below are:

- <b>create new collection</b> -- starts the sequence of steps for
  creating a new collection (directly in the main window).
- <b>open existing collection</b> -- opens a file dialog for
  loading an existing `.col` file.
- <b>recently opened</b> -- shows a table of the recently used
  collections with collection name, file name and date. A double
  click opens the collection directly.
- <b>quit program</b> -- exits Colman.

The start page also has two small buttons for increasing and
decreasing the font size (see also @ref tutorial_accessibility).

@tip
Colman can also be started from the command line with a `.col` file
as argument:

`./colman my_collection.col`

`--help` lists all options. `--trace-level 2` prints the requests of
the internet lookup to the console – useful for troubleshooting when
a title is not found.
@endtip

@image html 001_start.jpg "Colman start page"
@image latex 001_start.jpg "Colman start page"

@section tutorial_wizard Setting Up the Collection

Creating a new collection is a sequence of steps directly in the
main window (no separate window). <b>Next</b> takes you to the next
step, <b>back</b> (across the full width at the top) one step back.
The Enter key moves to the next field or triggers <b>Next</b>.

<b>Step 1 -- Collection name and organization</b>

Enter the basic data of your collection:

- <b>collection name</b> (required) -- e.g. "Textbook Collection".
  This name appears on library cards and barcodes.
- <b>organization</b> (required) -- e.g. "Springfield High School".
  Also appears on printouts.
- <b>responsible</b> (optional) -- name of the contact person.

@image html 002_new_collection_name.jpg "Step 1: collection name"
@image latex 002_new_collection_name.jpg "Step 1: collection name"


<b>Step 2 -- Logo</b>

Optionally, you can choose a logo that is printed on library cards.
Click <b>browse</b> to select an image file (SVG, PNG, JPG or XPM).
A preview of the logo is shown. SVG files are rendered
automatically at the printer's resolution when printing.

@image html 003_new_collection_logo.jpg "Step 2: choose a logo"
@image latex 003_new_collection_logo.jpg "Step 2: choose a logo"

<b>Step 3 -- Lending period</b>

Here you decide whether there is a default lending period:

- Enable <b>use default lending period</b>.
- Set the period in <b>year(s)</b>, <b>month(s)</b> and
  <b>day(s)</b>.

Without a lending period, lendings are created without a due date.

Below, you decide whether items may be reserved: <b>Allow
reservations</b> is enabled by default; in the field below you set
how many days a reservation remains valid (default: 14). Both values
can be changed later in the
@ref tutorial_settings_collection "collection settings".

@image html 004_new_collection_lending_period.jpg "Step 3: lending period"
@image latex 004_new_collection_lending_period.jpg "Step 3: lending period"

<b>Step 4 -- School items</b>

This step is aimed at school libraries:

- <b>default item type is school item</b> -- when enabled, new
  items automatically get the fields <b>subject</b>, <b>academic
  year</b> and a school item type (e.g. workbook, teacher's manual).

@tip
The <b>academic year</b> is entered as single grades or ranges,
separated by commas -- for example <tt>5-7, 9</tt>. This lets you
filter for a range of grades in the search later.
@endtip

@image html 005_new_collection_school_items.jpg "Step 4: school items"
@image latex 005_new_collection_school_items.jpg "Step 4: school items"

@section tutorial_wizard_encryption Setting Up Encryption (optional)

If the encryption driver is available, the step <b>Encrypt
Collection</b> follows. It lets you protect the new `.col` file with
a password from the start.

- <b>Without encryption</b>: leave the check box <b>Encrypt this
  collection</b> unchecked and click <b>Next</b>.
- <b>With encryption</b>: check the box. Enter a password into the
  fields <b>New password</b> and <b>Confirm</b>. A strength
  indicator shows the quality of the chosen password. Click
  <b>Next</b>.

Encryption can also be enabled or disabled later on the settings
page (see @ref tutorial_encryption).

@image html 007_new_collection_encryption.jpg "Step: setting up encryption"
@image latex 007_new_collection_encryption.jpg "Step: setting up encryption"

@note The file password is independent of the administrator
password. If you forget the file password, the `.col` file can no
longer be opened.

@section tutorial_admin_password Administrator Password

In the step <b>Set Administrator Password</b> you define the
password of the administrator account. Enter the password into both
fields (<b>Password</b> and <b>Confirm</b>) and click <b>Next</b>.

@image html 008_new_collection_admin_password.jpg "Setting the administrator password"
@image latex 008_new_collection_admin_password.jpg "Setting the administrator password"

@warning Remember this password well! It is required for all
administrative functions. The password is stored in the database as
an Argon2 hash and cannot be recovered in plain text.
@endwarning

@section tutorial_wizard_file Storage Location

In the last step you choose the file name and location of your
collection. The file gets the extension `.col`. <b>Finish</b>
creates the collection -- and, if chosen, encrypts it right away and
sets the administrator password.

If the file already exists, you are asked for confirmation first;
if you decline overwriting it, you stay on this step and can choose
a different name.

@image html 006_new_collection_file.jpg "Last step: storage location"
@image latex 006_new_collection_file.jpg "Last step: storage location"

@tip
Save the file in a location that is backed up regularly (e.g. a
network drive or cloud folder). Colman creates backups
automatically, but an additional backup does no harm.
@endtip

After the collection has been created, you are automatically logged
in as administrator and reach the
@ref tutorial_login_central "central navigation page".

---

@page tutorial_open_collection Opening an Existing Collection

To load an existing `.col` file, use one of the following options
on the start page:

- <b>open existing collection</b> -- opens a file dialog in which
  you select the desired `.col` file.
- <b>recently opened</b> -- a double click on an entry of the table
  opens the collection directly.

@image html 010_open_collection.jpg "Opening an existing collection"
@image latex 010_open_collection.jpg "Opening an existing collection"

For an unencrypted collection, the login page appears directly
afterwards.

@section tutorial_open_encrypted Opening an Encrypted Collection

If you enabled encryption when creating the collection or later in
the settings (see @ref tutorial_encryption), opening the `.col` file
first shows the page <b>Enter the database password</b>:

1. Enter the file password into the field <b>Password</b>.
2. Click <b>OK</b> or press <b>Enter</b>.
   - With the correct password, the collection is loaded and the
     login page appears.
   - With a wrong password, an error message (<b>Wrong password or
     database error.</b>) appears and the password field is
     cleared. You have three attempts in total.
3. After three failed attempts, the file is not opened.

Clicking <b>Back</b> returns you to the start page without opening
the file.

@image html 011_database_password.jpg "Entering the database password"
@image latex 011_database_password.jpg "Entering the database password"

@note The file password is independent of the administrator
password. If you have forgotten the file password, the `.col` file
can no longer be opened.

@section tutorial_new_db_due Notice of Due Returns When Opening

When you open a collection, Colman automatically checks whether
there are lendings due today. If so (on Fridays: due by Monday), the
page <b>Due Returns</b> appears right after logging in, with a table
of the affected items (due date, borrower, count, name). The print
button prints the list; <b>back</b> takes you to the central
navigation page.

---

@page tutorial_persons Adding Persons (Borrowers)

Before books can be lent, the persons (borrowers) must be added --
usually pupils or teachers.

@section tutorial_persons_navigate Navigation

Log in as administrator. On the <b>central navigation page</b>,
click <b>manage borrowers</b>.

@image html 009_central_page.jpg "Central navigation page"
@image latex 009_central_page.jpg "Central navigation page"

@section tutorial_persons_add Adding a New Person

The persons page has two areas: on the left the list of existing
persons with a search field, on the right the input form.

The <b>Class</b> selection next to the search field restricts the
list to a single class or to persons with <b>no class</b>.

1. In the right area, fill in at least the fields <b>last name</b>
   and <b>first name</b>, and enter a unique user name into the
   field <b>Username</b> at the top right. Colman suggests one from
   the name. Optionally, you can also enter title, email, telephone,
   street, city, zip, country, school class and remarks.

   @tip
   Preferably choose the class from the list. If you type a class
   that does not yet exist in the class management, Colman asks when
   saving whether it should be added to the list of classes. Typos
   such as "5 a" instead of "5a" are noticed immediately this way.
   @endtip
2. Click <b>insert as new person</b>. The button stays disabled as
   long as the name or the user name is missing. If the user name is
   already taken, an error message appears and the person is not
   added.
3. The person appears with their user name in the list on the left
   and automatically gets a barcode.
4. Right afterwards, the page <b>Set Initial Password</b> appears.
   Here you can optionally set a <b>password</b> (both fields must
   match). If you leave the password empty or leave the page with
   <b>back</b>, the person can only log in after a password has been
   set later (see @ref tutorial_passwords).

@image html 013_add_person.jpg "Adding a person"
@image latex 013_add_person.jpg "Adding a person"

@image html 014_initial_password.jpg "Setting the initial password"
@image latex 014_initial_password.jpg "Setting the initial password"

@tip
You can also type the <b>last name</b> into the search field on the
left first to check whether the person already exists. If so, a
warning appears when you nevertheless try to add a second person
with the same name.
@endtip

@tip
You can also set the user name of an existing person later directly
on the persons page: select the person → fill in the field
<b>Username</b> at the top right → click <b>Set Username</b>. If an
existing user name is to be changed, a confirmation dialog appears.
@endtip

@section tutorial_persons_permissions Setting Permissions

In the <b>Permissions</b> area (icon: owl with a lock) on the
persons page, you adjust the access rights of the selected person.
This requires the permission <b>Edit persons</b>, <b>Create
borrowers</b> or <b>Create data collectors</b>; you can only grant
permissions you hold yourself (see @ref tutorial_permissions). The area is embedded directly in the persons page
and shows all available permissions (e.g. the right to make
reservations) as check boxes.

@image html 016_permissions.jpg "Editing the permissions of a person"
@image latex 016_permissions.jpg "Editing the permissions of a person"

@note By default, new persons may only <b>Reserve media</b> and
<b>View personal data</b>. All further rights (e.g. <b>Record
lendings</b>) must be enabled explicitly.

@tip You can also delegate adding persons selectively: with
<b>Create borrowers</b> a person may only set up borrowers, with
<b>Create data collectors</b> only persons for acquisitions and
liquidations. The rule always applies: you can only grant rights
that you hold yourself.
@endtip

@section tutorial_persons_edit Editing a Person

1. Select the person in the list on the left. The fields on the
   right are filled with their data automatically.
2. Change the desired fields.
3. Click <b>change person</b>.

@section tutorial_persons_remove Removing a Person

1. Select the person in the list.
2. Click <b>remove person</b>.
3. Confirm the security prompt.

@warning A person can only be removed if they have no open
lendings. Return all lendings first if necessary.
@endwarning

@section tutorial_persons_clear Clearing the Fields

<b>clear fields</b> resets all input fields on the right and clears
the selection in the list. This is useful if you want to add a new
person after editing another one.

---

@page tutorial_person_barcodes Printing Library Cards

Every person automatically gets a barcode. From it, library cards
can be printed that are read with a barcode scanner at the lending
desk.

@section tutorial_person_barcodes_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>print IDs</b>.

@section tutorial_person_barcodes_print Printing

1. On the left, a searchable list of persons appears. Check the
   persons for whom you want to print cards. You can narrow the list
   with the search and then click <b>Select all</b>.

   The <b>Class</b> selection next to the search field shows only
   the persons of a particular class.

   @tip
   This is how you print the cards of a whole class at once: choose
   the class, click <b>Select all</b>, print.
   @endtip

   If you only want to see the most recently added persons, enable
   the check box <b>Only the last … created persons</b> next to the
   selection buttons and set the desired number in the spin box next
   to it. The list then only shows the correspondingly most recently
   added persons. By default, this restriction is off and all
   persons are shown.

   @tip
   Handy right after adding a new class or a group of new borrowers:
   restrict the list to the last <i>n</i> persons and quickly print
   exactly their cards with <b>Select all</b>.
   @endtip

2. Click <b>Print library cards</b>.
3. On the next page, choose the desired ID card layout in the field
   <b>Layout</b> at the top. Only the named ID card layouts you
   created yourself are offered (see @ref tutorial_label_layouts);
   the last one used is preselected. For a sheet layout, click the
   positions in the sheet preview that are to be printed on (at most
   as many as there are cards to print); the counter at the bottom
   right shows how many positions are selected, <b>Deselect all</b>
   clears the selection and <b>Select all</b> selects positions from
   the first one onward until the maximum is reached.
4. What appears on a card is defined by the ID card layout --
   usually:
   - the collection logo (if any; SVG logos are rendered at the
     printer's DPI),
   - the collection name and the organization,
   - the date of issue,
   - the full name of the person (bold),
   - the Code 128 barcode.
5. Click <b>Print</b>. For a sheet layout, the print dialog for
   choosing the printer appears the first time; a thermal label
   printer prints directly without a dialog. If more cards are
   selected than positions, you then choose the positions on the
   next sheet. <b>Cancel</b> aborts printing.

If <b>Show border</b> is enabled on the selection page, a thin
cutting line is printed around each card.

@image html 070_id_cards_print_01.jpg "Printing library cards"
@image latex 070_id_cards_print_01.jpg "Printing library cards"
@image html 071_id_cards_print_02.jpg "Printing library cards: layout and positions"
@image latex 071_id_cards_print_02.jpg "Printing library cards: layout and positions"
@image html 072_id_cards_print_recent.jpg "Printing ID cards: only recently created persons"
@image latex 072_id_cards_print_recent.jpg "Printing ID cards: only recently created persons"


@par Creating an ID card layout
ID card layouts are created in the <b>ID card layout editor</b>
(button <b>edit ID layout</b> on the central navigation page). There
you choose the printer and label format at the top and arrange the
zones for barcode, text and logo below: drag a zone to move it; drag
one of the white handles at the corners and edge centres to resize
it. A <b>double click</b> automatically extends a zone to the largest
possible area without overlapping the others. Printer, format and
arrangement together form <b>one</b> named layout, which you save
with the single <b>Save</b> button at the bottom right. This way you
can create several ID card layouts (see @ref tutorial_label_layouts).

@image html 021_id_card_layout.jpg "ID card layout editor"
@image latex 021_id_card_layout.jpg "ID card layout editor"

@tip
Print the cards on card stock or label paper so that they last.
Alternatively, you can laminate them.
@endtip

---

@page tutorial_classes Class Management

The class management lets you create, rename and delete school
classes and plan the class transitions for the new school year. The
class of a person (entered in the field <b>school class</b> in the
person management) is used as a filter in the view
@ref tutorial_open_lendings "Open Lendings".

@section tutorial_classes_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>class management</b>.

@section tutorial_classes_main Main Page: Managing Classes

The main page shows the list of all existing classes and, at the
top, the configured <b>Grade range</b> (e.g. 1 to 4 at a primary
school, 5 to 13 at a secondary school).

@image html 170_classes.jpg "Class management -- main page"
@image latex 170_classes.jpg "Class management -- main page"

The available actions:

- <b>Add</b> -- create a new class (an input field appears)
- <b>Remove</b> -- delete the selected class (after confirmation)
- <b>Rename</b> -- rename the selected class
- <b>Save</b> -- save the grade range
- <b>New school year</b> -- start the school year planning (see
  below)

@note Removing a class only removes the class name; the persons
assigned to it remain unchanged.

@section tutorial_classes_new_year Planning a New School Year

<b>New school year</b> opens a two-page assistant that automates
the class transitions:

<b>Page 1 -- Defining the transitions</b>

For each existing class, a table shows the current class name and
an editable field for the new name (e.g. "5a" → "6a"). Classes that
leave the grade range (graduating classes) get no new name and are
deleted. In the last row <b>(new class)</b> -- <b>Add new class</b>
creates further rows -- you enter new entry classes (e.g. "1a" for
the new first-graders). <b>Apply</b> takes you to page 2.

@image html 171_new_school_year_01.jpg "New school year -- page 1: defining the transitions"
@image latex 171_new_school_year_01.jpg "New school year -- page 1: defining the transitions"

<b>Page 2 -- Review and execute</b>

An overview table shows every person, grouped by class, with their
current and new class; persons without a new class (leavers) have an
empty field <b>New class</b>. Optionally, enable <b>Delete persons
without a new class</b> to remove pupils who have left from the
database automatically. <b>Execute</b> applies all changes at once.

@image html 172_new_school_year_02.jpg "New school year -- page 2: review and execute"
@image latex 172_new_school_year_02.jpg "New school year -- page 2: review and execute"

@warning Executing the change of school year cannot be undone.
Make a backup copy of the `.col` file beforehand.
@endwarning

---

@page tutorial_login Logging In

When you open an existing collection, the login page appears first.

@section tutorial_login_selection Choosing the Account

The login selection shows up to three buttons and an input field for
the barcode scanner:

- <b>Administrator Login</b> -- log in as administrator with full
  access to all management functions.
- <b>User Login</b> -- log in as a regular user with restricted
  access.
- <b>close collection</b> -- returns to the start page.
- <b>or scan ID card</b> -- input field for scanning a user's ID
  card (barcode scanner).

<b>User Login</b> only appears if the collection contains persons
besides the administrator. Below, <b>A-</b> and <b>A+</b> adjust the
font size.

@image html 012_login_selection.jpg "Login selection"
@image latex 012_login_selection.jpg "Login selection"


@section tutorial_login_admin Administrator Login

Using the button:

1. Click <b>Administrator Login</b>.
2. Enter the administrator password.
3. Click <b>Login</b>.

Using the barcode scanner: scan the administrator's ID card barcode
on the login page -- the administrator login page opens immediately.

With a wrong password, <b>Wrong password!</b> appears and input is
blocked for three seconds.


@section tutorial_login_user User Login

Using the button:

1. Click <b>User Login</b>.
2. Enter your <b>user name</b> or scan your <b>ID card barcode</b>
   into the field <b>Username or barcode</b>.
3. Enter your <b>password</b>.
4. Click <b>Login</b>.

Using the barcode scanner on the login page: scan the ID card
barcode directly on the login selection page -- the password page
opens immediately with the barcode field filled in and the focus on
the password field.

With a wrong user name, barcode or password, an error message
appears and input is blocked for three seconds.

@note Users need a password to log in. The administrator sets it in
the person management (see @ref tutorial_passwords). No user name is
required for the barcode login -- the ID card barcode is sufficient.

@section tutorial_login_central Central Navigation Page

After logging in successfully, the <b>central navigation page</b>
appears. It is the heart of Colman and gives access to all
functions.

The buttons are arranged in four rows by task area -- the most
frequently needed at the top; within a row they follow the usual
order of work. The administrator sees all buttons except <b>My
reservations</b>. Regular users only see the buttons for which they
have the permission named in brackets (see
@ref tutorial_persons_permissions); hidden buttons leave no gap, and
rows without a visible button are omitted entirely.

<b>Row 1 -- Lending desk</b>
- <b>lend item</b> (<b>Record lendings</b>); users who may only
  <b>Reserve media</b> see <b>reserve item</b> here
- <b>group lending</b> (<b>Record lendings</b>) -- one copy for each
  member of a group, e.g. a class (see @ref tutorial_group_lending)
- <b>return item</b> (<b>Record returns</b>)
- <b>open lendings</b> (always visible; users without <b>View
  person lists</b> only see their own lendings)
- <b>My reservations</b> (only users with <b>Reserve media</b>)
- <b>Search</b> (<b>View item lists</b>)
- <b>Barcodes</b> (always visible) -- read a barcode with the camera
  (see @ref tutorial_barcode_dispatch); only active if a camera is
  connected

<b>Row 2 -- Stock</b>
- <b>acquire</b> (<b>Record acquisitions</b>)
- <b>liquidate</b> (<b>Record liquidations</b>)
- <b>print labels</b> (<b>Print labels</b>)
- <b>print signatures</b> (<b>Print signature labels</b>)
- <b>view history</b> (<b>View history</b>)

<b>Row 3 -- Persons and own account</b>
- <b>manage borrowers</b> (<b>Edit persons</b>, <b>Create
  borrowers</b> or <b>Create data collectors</b>)
- <b>print IDs</b> (<b>Print ID cards</b>)
- <b>class management</b> (<b>Manage classes</b>)
- <b>Change Passwords</b> (administrator only) or <b>Change
  Password</b> (users, for their own account)
- <b>log out</b> (always visible)

<b>Row 4 -- Setup</b>
- <b>Settings</b> (always visible)
- <b>manage categories</b> (<b>Edit categories</b>)
- <b>edit ID layout</b>, <b>edit item card layout</b> and <b>edit
  signature layout</b> (each <b>Edit ID card layout</b>)
- <b>calibrate printer</b> (administrator only)

The status bar at the bottom shows who is currently logged in.

@image html 009_central_page.jpg "Central navigation page (administrator)"
@image latex 009_central_page.jpg "Central navigation page (administrator)"

@image html 018_central_page_restricted.jpg "Central navigation page (user)"
@image latex 018_central_page_restricted.jpg "Central navigation page (user)"


---

@page tutorial_logout Logging Out

When you have finished your work or another user is to log in, you
can log out without quitting the program.

@section tutorial_logout_steps Procedure

1. On the central navigation page, click <b>log out</b>.
2. A security prompt appears: <b>Really log out?</b>
3. Confirm with <b>Yes</b>. <b>No</b> returns you to the central
   navigation page without logging out.

After logging out, the login selection page appears (see
@ref tutorial_login_selection). The database remains open, so that
another user or the administrator can log in right away.

@image html 160_logout.jpg "Logging out: security prompt"
@image latex 160_logout.jpg "Logging out: security prompt"


@tip
Log out when you leave the lending desk or take a break. This
prevents anyone else from making changes under your name.
@endtip

---

@page tutorial_items Adding Items (Media)

Items -- usually books -- are added with the function
<b>acquire</b>. Colman guides you through it in two steps: first
you select an existing item or enter a new one, then you enter the
number of copies acquired.

@section tutorial_items_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>acquire</b>.

@section tutorial_items_step1 Step 1: Selecting or Entering an Item

The page <b>Step 1: Please select the item!</b> has two parts: on
the left the list of existing media of the selected media type, on
the right the data of the selected or new item.

- The <b>Type</b> selection defines the media type (e.g. "Book",
  "DVD", "Board game"). The default type from the collection
  settings is preselected. The list on the left only shows media of
  this type, and the fields matching the type appear on the right.
  Data already entered is kept when the type is changed.
- Below the list, the number of media shown is displayed. For very
  large collections, <b>Load more...</b> appears -- a click loads
  further media.
- <b>Clear fields</b> clears the selection and empties the fields on
  the right.

@image html 030_acquisition_01.jpg "acquire -- step 1: selecting the item"
@image latex 030_acquisition_01.jpg "acquire -- step 1: selecting the item"

<b>Searching for an existing item (further copies):</b>
- Enter the beginning of the name, the Colman barcode or the ISBN
  into the field <b>name/barcode</b>, or scan the barcode. The
  search starts as soon as you press <b>Enter</b> or leave the
  field; the list then only shows the matching media. If there is
  exactly one hit, it is selected automatically.
- Select the desired item in the list. Its data appears on the
  right (read-only). <b>Acquire more copies</b> takes you to
  @ref tutorial_items_step2 "step 2".

@image html 031_acquisition_02.jpg "acquire -- step 1: existing item found"
@image latex 031_acquisition_02.jpg "acquire -- step 1: existing item found"

<b>Adding a new item:</b>
- Fill in the fields on the right (see below). As soon as a name has
  been entered, the button <b>As new item</b> appears; it takes you
  to @ref tutorial_items_step2 "step 2".
- Or enter an ISBN or product barcode (EAN/UPC) into the field
  <b>name/barcode</b>: if Colman does not find an existing entry, an
  internet look-up starts automatically and its result is filled
  into the fields on the right.
- Or enter a <b>title as free text</b>: if Colman does not find an
  existing entry, the
  @ref tutorial_items_title_search "internet title search" opens
  automatically for the media types book, CD, DVD and Blu-ray (for
  board games the BoardGameGeek search, if it is enabled in the
  program settings).

<b>Entering the item data</b>

When adding a new item, you fill in the data:

- <b>name/title</b> (required) -- title of the book, DVD etc.
- <b>subject</b> and <b>academic year</b> -- for school items.
- <b>is school item</b> -- check this if the item is a textbook.
- <b>signature top</b> / <b>signature bottom</b> -- optional
  labelling for the spine label (e.g. subject area at the top,
  author abbreviation at the bottom).
- <b>description</b> -- short description or summary.
- <b>keywords</b> -- topical keywords.
- Additional type-specific fields (depending on the media type).

@tip
The signatures of the most recently acquired new item are suggested
automatically for the next acquisition. If you enter several books
of the same subject area, you only need to type them once.
@endtip

<b>ISBN look-up (recommended):</b>

Enter the ISBN into the corresponding field or read the ISBN barcode
on the back of the book with a <b>barcode scanner</b>. If the ISBN
look-up is enabled in the program settings (see @ref tutorial_prefs),
Colman automatically searches the internet for title, author,
publication date and further details. In many cases you then do not
need to enter anything by hand.

Alternatively, click <b>Internet search</b> to look up an ISBN
already entered.

@anchor tutorial_items_title_search
<b>Internet title search (books, CDs, DVDs, Blu-rays):</b>

Even without an ISBN or barcode, you can take media data from the
internet. Click <b>Internet search</b> with an empty ISBN or EAN
field -- or enter a title as free text into the field
<b>name/barcode</b>. The page <b>Title Search</b> opens:

1. The title entered is already filled into the search field and
   the search starts automatically; <b>Search</b> searches again
   with a changed term.
2. Colman queries all suitable services at the same time --
   depending on the media type:
   - <b>Book</b>: OpenLibrary, Google Books and the configured
     library catalogues (SRU servers, e.g. the German National
     Library)
   - <b>CD</b>: MusicBrainz and the SRU servers
   - <b>DVD / Blu-ray</b>: the SRU servers
3. For each entry, the list of hits shows the title and -- if
   available -- the author or artist as well as the year and the
   source, e.g. "The Hobbit — J. R. R. Tolkien (1937) — Open
   Library". This makes titles with the same name easier to tell
   apart.
4. Select the matching hit and click <b>Apply</b> (or double-click
   the entry). The fields of the item -- such as author, publisher,
   publication date, director or artist -- are filled in
   automatically.

Which services are queried is defined in the program settings (see
@ref tutorial_prefs).

@tip
The results of the library catalogues are filtered by the selected
media type: a DVD search, for example, automatically drops books
with the same title. Still, it is worth looking at the source of the
hits -- the same edition can be reported by several services.
@endtip

<!-- @image html 037_acquisition_title_search.jpg "acquire -- internet title search" -->
<!-- @image latex 037_acquisition_title_search.jpg "acquire -- internet title search" -->

<b>Cover image:</b>

- Click <b>browse</b> next to the field <b>thumbnail</b> to load an
  image from disk, or <b>scan</b> to take it with the camera.
  <b>zoom</b> shows the image at full size.
- If the automatic thumbnail download is enabled in the program
  settings, the cover image is downloaded automatically during the
  ISBN look-up.
- <b>remove</b> detaches a cover image from the item, for example if
  the automatic search delivered a wrong image.
- <b>Amazon</b> fetches a cover image online: if the ISBN look-up
  found a cover URL, that image is used directly; otherwise (or if
  the item already has a cover image) an Amazon.de search for the
  ISBN/EAN or the item name opens inside Colman. Click a product
  image to adopt it as cover image; <b>back</b> returns without a
  change.

@anchor tutorial_cover_copyright
<b>Which cover images may you use?</b>

Cover images are protected by copyright. The rights usually belong to
the publisher, illustrator or photographer -- not to Amazon or the
other services from which Colman downloads the images. Amazon itself
grants no rights to the content of its pages beyond personal,
non-commercial use; collecting, storing or otherwise re-using the
content is not covered by its
<a href="https://www.amazon.de/gp/help/customer/display.html?nodeId=201909000">conditions
of use</a>. Whether you may store an image in your collection
therefore depends on copyright law alone. Under German law
(other countries have similar rules):

- <b>Private collection:</b> copies for purely private use are
  permitted (§ 53 UrhG), as long as the collection is not made
  available to others.
- <b>Library:</b> publicly accessible libraries without commercial
  purposes may reproduce works from their own stock for indexing and
  cataloguing (§ 60e para. 1 UrhG) and show them in their catalogue.
  This only covers items the library actually owns.
- <b>Publication:</b> making the images available on a public web
  page, in a newsletter or similar requires the consent of the rights
  holder.

The safest way is always to photograph or scan the cover of your own
copy (<b>scan</b>). This information is no legal advice; if in doubt,
ask the school authority or the rights holder.

@tip
Above the Amazon page, Colman shows a short reminder of these rules
with a link to the Amazon conditions of use.
@endtip

@image html 032_acquisition_03.jpg "acquire -- entering the item data"
@image latex 032_acquisition_03.jpg "acquire -- entering the item data"

@image html 033_acquisition_04.jpg "acquire -- type-specific fields"
@image latex 033_acquisition_04.jpg "acquire -- type-specific fields"

@section tutorial_items_step2 Step 2: Number of Copies

The page <b>Step 2: Edit acquisition details</b> shows the name of
the item.

1. Enter the number of new copies into the field <b>How many items
   have been acquired?</b> (default: 1).
2. Click <b>finish</b>. The acquisition is saved immediately; Colman
   automatically records the current date and time as the time of
   acquisition.

<b>back</b> returns to step 1 without saving anything.

@image html 034_acquisition_05.jpg "acquire -- step 2: number of copies"
@image latex 034_acquisition_05.jpg "acquire -- step 2: number of copies"


@section tutorial_items_done Completion

After saving, the question <b>Further acquisitions?</b> appears with
the following options:

- <b>yes</b> -- enter another item (back to step 1).
- <b>no</b> -- back to the central navigation page.

Directly below are three print buttons for the newly added item:

- <b>Print item card</b> -- opens the label printing page with the
  new item already selected. From there, the book label can be
  printed. <b>back</b> takes you back to this completion page, so
  that you can print signature labels afterwards.
- <b>Print upper signature</b> -- prints a signature label with the
  upper signature of the new item.
- <b>Print lower signature</b> -- prints a signature label with the
  lower signature of the new item.

@image html 036_acquisition_07.jpg "acquire -- completion with print options"
@image latex 036_acquisition_07.jpg "acquire -- completion with print options"

@note The signature print buttons use the most recently used
signature layout. If it is a connected thermal label printer, it
prints immediately; otherwise the signature printing page opens for
choosing the layout. If no signature layout has been created yet,
printing is not possible. For details on setting up layouts see
@ref tutorial_label_layouts "Editing Label Layouts".

---

@page tutorial_item_barcodes Printing Labels for Items

Every item automatically gets a barcode. From it, labels can be
printed that you stick onto the item (e.g. the spine of a book).

@section tutorial_item_barcodes_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>print labels</b>.

@section tutorial_item_barcodes_select Selecting Items

1. Search for the desired item in the search field
   (<b>barcode/ISBN/name</b>).
2. In the list, check every item for which a label is to be printed,
   or click <b>Select all</b> (<b>Deselect all</b> removes all
   checks). Clicking an entry shows its data on the right.
3. Click <b>Print labels</b>.

If you only want to see the most recently acquired media, enable the
check box <b>Only the last … acquired items</b> next to the
selection buttons and set the desired number in the spin box next to
it. The table then only shows the correspondingly most recently
acquired items. By default, this restriction is off and all items
are shown.

If you need a fixed period instead, enable the check box
<b>Acquired between</b> and set the start and end date in the two
date fields next to it. The table then only shows items acquired in
this period (both days included). The two restrictions are mutually
exclusive: enabling one switches the other off.

@tip
Handy after a larger delivery: restrict the list to the last
<i>n</i> acquisitions or enter the delivery period under
<b>Acquired between</b>, and quickly select exactly the newly
arrived media for labelling with <b>Select all</b>.
@endtip

@image html 080_item_labels_print_01.jpg "Printing labels: selecting items"
@image latex 080_item_labels_print_01.jpg "Printing labels: selecting items"


@section tutorial_item_barcodes_layout Layout and Positions

After clicking <b>Print labels</b>, the printing page opens:

- Choose the desired item card layout from the drop-down
  <b>Layout</b>. Only the named item card layouts you created
  yourself are offered (see @ref tutorial_label_layouts); the last
  one used is preselected. There is no button for editing layouts
  here -- layouts are created in the item card layout editor.
- For a sheet layout, the preview shows the positions on the sheet.
  Click the positions that are to be printed on (at most as many as
  there are labels to print) -- this way partially used sheets can
  be used, too. The counter at the bottom right shows how many
  positions are selected; <b>Deselect all</b> clears the selection,
  <b>Select all</b> selects positions from the first one onward until
  the maximum is reached.
  For a thermal label printer, there is no position selection.

In the spin box <b>Copies</b>, set how many identical labels are to
be printed per selected item (default: 1). For a sheet layout, the
number of positions to choose increases accordingly; a thermal label
printer outputs the corresponding number of labels per item.

Optional: enable <b>Show border</b> to print a thin cutting line
around each label.

@image html 081_item_labels_print_02.jpg "Printing labels: layout and positions"
@image latex 081_item_labels_print_02.jpg "Printing labels: layout and positions"

@section tutorial_item_barcodes_print Printing

Click <b>Print</b>. For a sheet layout, the print dialog for
choosing the printer appears the first time; a thermal label printer
prints directly without a dialog. If more items are selected than
positions, you then choose the positions on the next sheet.
<b>Cancel</b> aborts printing.

What appears on a label is defined by the item card layout -- e.g.
organization, title, author, ISBN, signature and the Code 128
barcode (see @ref tutorial_label_layouts).

---

@page tutorial_label_layouts Editing Label Layouts

Colman prints labels in three categories, each with its own visual
editor on the central navigation page:

- <b>ID cards</b> (library cards) -- editor <b>edit ID layout</b>.
- <b>Item cards</b> (barcode labels for items) -- editor <b>edit
  item card layout</b>.
- <b>Signature labels</b> (spine labels) -- editor <b>edit signature
  layout</b>.

@section tutorial_label_layouts_what What Is a Layout?

A <b>layout</b> combines everything that makes up a print job: the
<b>printer</b>, the <b>geometry</b> (label size or sheet format with
columns, rows, margins and gaps) and the <b>arrangement of the
elements</b> on the label (barcode, text, logo or signature text).
Each layout gets a <b>name</b> and belongs to exactly one category --
a layout created in the ID card editor only appears when printing ID
cards and cannot be used for book labels.

You can create any number of layouts -- for example for different
printers or labels, each with its own arrangement of elements. For
common label sheets (e.g. Avery Zweckform, Herma) and for detected
thermal label printers (registered in CUPS, e.g. Brother QL, Dymo
LabelWriter, or connected directly via USB -- these appear with the
suffix "(USB)"), templates are available as a starting point -- but
only in the editors, not in the print dialog.

@note So far, two thermal label printers have been tested with Colman
and are recognized automatically: the <b>Vevor Y486</b> and the
<b>Munbyn RW403B</b>. Only these two are offered when they are
connected directly via USB without being set up in the print system.
Other label printers registered in CUPS are detected by their name and
label-sized paper formats and printed via CUPS, but have not been
tested. Help with supporting and testing further models is very
welcome -- and if you would like to lend the author a specific
printer model for this, please get in touch via an issue on GitHub
:-)

@section tutorial_label_layouts_editing Creating a Layout

The procedure is the same in all three editors:

1. Open the editor from the central navigation page.
2. At the top, choose a template or one of your own layouts as a
   starting point in the field <b>Layout</b>, or click <b>New</b>
   for an empty start. The selection immediately loads geometry
   <b>and</b> arrangement into the preview.
3. At the top, define the <b>Printer</b> and the geometry (label
   size for thermal printers; columns, rows, dimensions, margins and
   gaps for sheets). For thermal printers, you can switch the field
   <b>Printer</b> to another detected label printer at any time,
   even for a saved layout, without losing size and arrangement.
4. Arrange the zones in the preview below: dragging moves them, the
   white handles resize them, a double click extends a zone to the
   maximum.
5. Enter a <b>Name</b> and click the single <b>Save</b> button at
   the bottom right. Printer, geometry and arrangement are saved
   together under this name. <b>Delete</b> removes one of your own
   layouts (templates cannot be deleted).

@image html 022_book_card_layout.jpg "Item card layout editor"
@image latex 022_book_card_layout.jpg "Item card layout editor"
@image html 023_signature_layout.jpg "Signature layout editor"
@image latex 023_signature_layout.jpg "Signature layout editor"

@note When printing (ID cards, book labels, signatures), you only
choose an existing, named layout of the respective category; the
last one used is preselected. There is no button for editing layouts
in the print dialog.

@section tutorial_label_layouts_options Printer Fine-Tuning

The editor offers additional settings, saved per layout:

- <b>Bar width reduction</b> -- how much narrower each black barcode
  bar is drawn when printing (separately for each printer).
- <b>Print darkness</b> (thermal printers only) -- how dark the
  print is; higher values print darker. <b>Printer default</b>
  leaves the printer's setting unchanged.
- <b>Rotate output by 180°</b> (thermal printers only) -- turns the
  printed content upside down in case the labels come out of the
  printer upside down.

@tip On a thermal printer, the heated dots bleed: black bars become
wider, the white gaps narrower, and with too dark a setting narrow
bars merge into a thick line -- the barcode becomes unreadable. Set
a small <b>Bar width reduction</b> (typically 0.1--0.2 mm) for a
thermal printer layout so that the bars have the correct width again
after bleeding. For laser or inkjet printers, leave the value at 0.
@endtip

@tip The right <b>Print darkness</b> depends on the thermal paper
used: coloured paper usually needs a lower value, white paper a
higher one. Ideally, create one layout per paper type (such as
"Thermal white" and "Thermal coloured"), each with a suitable
darkness.
@endtip

@image html 024_bar_width_reduction.jpg "Bar width reduction in the layout editor"
@image latex 024_bar_width_reduction.jpg "Bar width reduction in the layout editor"

---

@page tutorial_signature_print Printing Signature Labels

Signature labels are narrow spine labels that show where an item is
shelved in the library (e.g. "Fic" for fiction by author, "Sci 500"
for science books in Dewey class 500).

@section tutorial_signature_print_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>print signatures</b>.

@section tutorial_signature_print_layout Page Layout

The signature printing page has two columns:

<b>Left column -- list of items</b>

Shows all items of the collection; the field above it filters the
list by name. Click an item to copy its signatures into the combo
boxes on the right.

<b>Right column -- print controls</b>

- Group <b>Upper signature</b>:
  - combo box with all saved upper signature categories; free text
    input is possible.
  - counter for the desired number of labels.
  - button <b>Print upper signature</b>.
- Group <b>Lower signature</b>:
  - combo box with all saved lower signature categories (independent
    of the upper signature); free text input is possible.
  - counter for the desired number of labels.
  - button <b>Print lower signature</b>.

No layout is chosen on this first page yet -- that happens only
after clicking one of the print buttons.

@image html 090_signature_print_01.jpg "Printing signature labels"
@image latex 090_signature_print_01.jpg "Printing signature labels"

@section tutorial_signature_print_workflow Printing

After clicking <b>Print upper signature</b> or <b>Print lower
signature</b>, a second page opens on which you first choose the
<b>Layout</b>. Only the named signature layouts you created yourself
are offered (see @ref tutorial_label_layouts); the last one used is
preselected. If no signature layout has been created yet, the page
points this out and printing stays disabled.

<b>Thermal label printer</b>

If the selected layout is a thermal printer, Colman prints the
desired number of labels directly to the printer without any further
dialog. If the printer cannot be reached, a warning asks you to
connect the printer. If the printer reports an empty label roll, you
are warned as well: insert a new roll and simply start printing
again. If the roll runs out in the middle of a batch, Colman also
points this out -- the printer remembers the rest of the job and
continues printing on its own after the new roll has been inserted.
Not every model can report its paper status; for such printers (e.g.
Munbyn RW403B) these warnings are not available.

@tip For crisp labels, Colman prefers to print directly over the USB
connection to the thermal printer. For this, your user account on
Linux must be a member of the group `lp` (once:
`sudo usermod -aG lp username`, then log in again). Without this
permission, printing goes through the print system and the result
may be grainier.
@endtip

<b>Regular page printer</b>

For a sheet layout, you continue on the same page:

1. In a preview of the label sheet, click the desired positions (the
   selected fields show a text preview).
2. Click <b>Print</b>. The first time, a print dialog for choosing
   the printer appears.
3. The printed number is subtracted from the remaining number. If
   labels are left, you can select further positions on the next
   sheet.
4. Repeat steps 1--3 until all labels are printed, or click
   <b>Cancel</b>.

@image html 091_signature_print_02.jpg "Signature labels: choosing positions"
@image latex 091_signature_print_02.jpg "Signature labels: choosing positions"

@note Signature labels can also be printed directly at the end of an
acquisition -- see @ref tutorial_items_done "Completion".

---

@page tutorial_liquidation Removing Items (Liquidation)

A liquidation reduces the stock of an item, e.g. when it is weeded
out, lost or damaged.

@section tutorial_liquidation_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>liquidate</b>.

@section tutorial_liquidation_step1 Step 1: Selecting the Item

1. Enter the name, barcode or ISBN into the search field
   <b>barcode/name</b>.
2. The table only shows items with available stock.
3. Select the desired item and click <b>next</b>. Alternatively, a
   barcode scanner identifies the item automatically.

Below the table, the number of loaded entries is shown. For very
large collections, <b>Load more...</b> appears -- a click loads
further media.

@image html 040_liquidation_01.jpg "Liquidation -- step 1: selecting the item"
@image latex 040_liquidation_01.jpg "Liquidation -- step 1: selecting the item"

@section tutorial_liquidation_step2 Step 2: Number

1. The item name and the number of available copies are shown for
   confirmation.
2. Enter the number into the field <b>how many items have been
   liquidated?</b> (at most as many as are available).
3. Click <b>finish</b>. Colman automatically records the current
   date and time as the time of liquidation. <b>previous</b> returns
   to step 1.

@image html 041_liquidation_02.jpg "Liquidation -- step 2: number"
@image latex 041_liquidation_02.jpg "Liquidation -- step 2: number"

@section tutorial_liquidation_step3 Step 3: Completion

The question <b>Further liquidations?</b> appears:

- <b>yes</b> -- liquidate another item.
- <b>no</b> -- back to the central navigation page.

@image html 042_liquidation_03.jpg "Liquidation -- step 3: completion"
@image latex 042_liquidation_03.jpg "Liquidation -- step 3: completion"

---

@page tutorial_barcode_dispatch Quick Access with a Barcode Scanner

The quickest way to start a transaction is to scan a barcode
directly on the central navigation page -- without clicking a button
first.

@section tutorial_barcode_dispatch_how How It Works

Point the barcode scanner at the barcode of the item or of the
library card and trigger the scanner. Colman detects the type of
barcode automatically and takes you to the right page:

<b>Item barcode:</b>

- If the item is available and nobody has currently borrowed it,
  Colman switches directly to the <b>lending page</b>. The item is
  already selected.
- If all copies are lent, Colman switches directly to the <b>return
  page</b>. Item and borrower are already preselected.
- If there are both available and lent copies, a selection page
  appears (see below).

<b>ISBN barcode (back of the book):</b>

- If the book is not yet in the collection, Colman switches directly
  to the <b>acquisition page</b>. The ISBN is already filled in.

<b>Library card barcode:</b>

- Colman switches directly to the <b>lending page</b> and selects the
  person automatically.

@section tutorial_barcode_dispatch_multi Selection Page for Several Options

If the same barcode allows several actions (e.g. a book with copies
that are both available and lent), the page <b>What do you want to
do?</b> appears.

1. For each possible action, the page shows an entry with
   - the action icon (as on the central navigation page),
   - the type of action (<b>Lending</b>, <b>Return</b> etc.) and
   - a short description (item name, author if any, for returns also
     the borrower's name and the lending date).
2. Click the desired entry.
3. Colman immediately switches to the matching page with the fields
   filled in.

<b>back</b> takes you back to the central navigation page.

@par Typical desk workflow
In the morning, one pupil returns a book and another wants to borrow
the same one: scan the book twice -- the first scan shows the
selection page (return / lend), the second scan (after the return)
puts the book straight into the lending.

@page tutorial_lending Lending

Lending is done in several steps. The transaction links a person to
an item and reduces the available stock.

@section tutorial_lending_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>lend item</b>.

@section tutorial_lending_step1 Step 1: Selecting the Person

1. On the left, a searchable list of persons appears. Type the name
   or scan the barcode of the library card.
2. Select the person.
3. Click <b>next</b>.

@image html 050_lending_01.jpg "Lending -- step 1: selecting the person"
@image latex 050_lending_01.jpg "Lending -- step 1: selecting the person"

@section tutorial_lending_extend Extending the Due Date

If a person wants to keep an item longer, you extend the due date
without taking the item back and lending it again:

1. Select the person in step 1.
2. Click <b>extend due date...</b> (bottom left).
3. The dialog shows all open lendings of the person with due date,
   class, count and name. All lendings are preselected; Ctrl-click
   or Shift-click changes the selection.
4. Check the <b>new due date</b>. The suggestion always extends: it
   starts from the latest current due date of the selected lendings
   (from today for lendings already overdue) and adds the default
   lending period from the collection settings, or 14 days if none
   is set. You can choose an earlier date by hand, but not one in
   the past.
5. Click <b>Extend</b>.

Only users with the permission <b>Record lendings</b> (and the
administrator) see the button.

Further ways to extend:

- <b>Open Lendings</b> (@ref tutorial_open_lendings_extend): select
  lendings in the list -- also of several persons, e.g. a whole
  class -- and extend them together.
- <b>Return, step 4</b> (@ref tutorial_return_step4): extends
  exactly the lending currently selected.

Where the lendings are already determined, the dialog only asks for
the new due date.

@image html 055_lending_extend.jpg "Lending -- extending the due date"
@image latex 055_lending_extend.jpg "Lending -- extending the due date"

@section tutorial_lending_step2 Step 2: Selecting the Item

1. Enter the name, barcode or ISBN into the search field. Media of
   which no copy is available any more are shown in grey and cannot
   be selected.
2. Select the item.
3. Click <b>next</b>.

Below the table, the number of loaded entries is shown. For very
large collections, <b>Load more...</b> appears -- a click loads
further media.

@image html 051_lending_02.jpg "Lending -- step 2: selecting the item"
@image latex 051_lending_02.jpg "Lending -- step 2: selecting the item"

@section tutorial_lending_step3 Step 3: Lending Details

The names of the person and of the item are shown. Check or change:

- <b>Lend</b> / <b>Reserve</b> -- radio buttons; visible if the user
  has the reserve permission. <b>Lend</b> is selected by default.
- <b>number</b> -- how many copies are lent (default: 1; at most as
  many as are available and not reserved for others).
- <b>with duedate</b> -- check box; only if a lending period is
  configured. When checked, the due date is set.
- <b>remarks</b> -- free text field.

Colman automatically records the current date and time as the time
of lending. Click <b>next</b>.

@image html 052_lending_03.jpg "Lending -- step 3: details"
@image latex 052_lending_03.jpg "Lending -- step 3: details"

@section tutorial_lending_step4 Step 4: Summary

A summary shows all details for checking: person, item, number, due
date and remarks.

Click <b>finish</b> to save the lending.

@image html 053_lending_04.jpg "Lending -- step 4: summary"
@image latex 053_lending_04.jpg "Lending -- step 4: summary"

@section tutorial_lending_step5 Step 5: Completion

The question <b>Further lendings for person?</b> appears:

- <b>yes</b> -- lend another item to the same person (back to step
  2: selecting the item).
- <b>no</b> -- back to the central navigation page.

@image html 054_lending_05.jpg "Lending -- step 5: completion"
@image latex 054_lending_05.jpg "Lending -- step 5: completion"

@par Barcode scanner workflow
The quickest way: scan the person's library card (step 1), scan the
barcode label of the book (step 2) and confirm (steps 3-4). In many
cases, the whole lending is done in a few seconds.

---

@page tutorial_group_lending Group Lending

With <b>group lending</b>, every member of a group -- typically a
class -- receives one copy of the same item, e.g. a class set of
workbooks. Instead of recording the lending for each person
separately, you select the item first and then tick all borrowers
in a list.

Only users with the <b>Record lendings</b> permission (and the
administrator) see this button.

@section tutorial_group_lending_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>group lending</b>.

@section tutorial_group_lending_step1 Step 1: Selecting the Item

1. Type the name, barcode or ISBN in the search field, or scan the
   label of a copy. Items with no copy left are grayed out.
2. Select the item.
3. Click <b>next</b>. A scanned barcode leads directly to step 2.

@image html 056_group_lending_01.jpg "Group lending -- step 1: selecting the item"
@image latex 056_group_lending_01.jpg "Group lending -- step 1: selecting the item"

@section tutorial_group_lending_step2 Step 2: Ticking the Borrowers

The selected item is shown above the list. Every ticked person
receives <b>one</b> copy.

- <b>Class</b> -- restricts the list to one class.
- <b>name/barcode</b> -- filters the list by last or first name. If
  you scan a <b>library card</b> here, the person is ticked right
  away; when handing out in the classroom you can simply scan all
  cards one after the other.
- <b>Select all</b> / <b>Deselect all</b> -- only affect the persons
  currently visible. Ticked persons stay ticked when you switch the
  class, so several classes can be combined.

At the bottom left, Colman shows how many persons are selected and
how many copies are left. You can tick at most as many persons as
copies are available and not reserved for others. If the stock does
not suffice for everybody on <b>Select all</b>, persons are ticked
from top to bottom until no copy is left.

The <b>Note</b> column shows:

- <b>already has this item</b> -- the person still has a copy on
  loan; the row is grayed out and cannot be ticked, so nothing is
  handed out twice.
- <b>has reserved ... copy(ies)</b> -- the person receives their
  reserved copy; the reservation is reduced accordingly.

Click <b>next</b>.

@image html 057_group_lending_02.jpg "Group lending -- step 2: ticking the borrowers"
@image latex 057_group_lending_02.jpg "Group lending -- step 2: ticking the borrowers"

@section tutorial_group_lending_step3 Step 3: Details

As with a single lending, you set the <b>due date</b> (suggested
from the collection's default lending period) and may enter
<b>remarks</b>. Both apply to all lendings of the group. Click
<b>next</b>.

@section tutorial_group_lending_step4 Step 4: Summary

The summary shows the item, number, due date, remarks and the names
of all borrowers. Click <b>finish</b>.

Colman stores all lendings at once -- either all or none. If someone
at another workstation has meanwhile lent copies so that they no
longer suffice, a message appears and you return to step 2.

@image html 058_group_lending_03.jpg "Group lending -- step 4: summary"
@image latex 058_group_lending_03.jpg "Group lending -- step 4: summary"

@section tutorial_group_lending_done Completion and Hand-out List

- <b>Print hand-out list...</b> -- prints a list with number, name,
  class and a signature column for each person, e.g. to sign when
  handing out.
- <b>Further group lending</b> -- starts again with step 1.
- <b>done</b> -- back to the central navigation page.

Every person gets a lending of their own. It appears individually in
the history and in the open lendings and is returned like any other
lending -- for the whole group at once with a group return (see
@ref tutorial_return_group).

@image html 059_group_lending_04.jpg "Group lending -- completion"
@image latex 059_group_lending_04.jpg "Group lending -- completion"

---

@page tutorial_reservations Reservations

A <b>reservation</b> secures a person the right to borrow an item
next. Reserved copies are deducted when the available number is
calculated for other users.

@section tutorial_reservations_permission Prerequisite

Reservations are only possible if the user has the permission
<b>Reserve media</b>. The administrator grants this permission in
the person management (@ref tutorial_persons_permissions).

@section tutorial_reservations_create Creating a Reservation

A reservation is created in the same procedure as a lending:

1. On the central navigation page, click <b>lend item</b> (or
   <b>reserve item</b>).
2. Select the person in step 1.
3. Select the item in step 2.
4. In step 3, choose the radio button <b>Reserve</b> instead of
   <b>Lend</b>.
5. Optionally set an expiry date.
6. Complete the transaction.

@section tutorial_reservations_view Viewing Reservations

Active reservations of an item are visible in the detail view of the
search page (@ref tutorial_search) under <b>Active
Reservations</b>.

@section tutorial_reservations_cancel Cancelling Your Own Reservation

Users can cancel their own reservations at any time from the central
navigation page:

1. On the central navigation page, click <b>My reservations</b>.
2. The page shows all your own active reservations with item name
   and expiry date.
3. In the desired row, click <b>Cancel reservation</b> to delete the
   reservation immediately.

@tip The button <b>My reservations</b> is only visible to users with
the permission <b>Reserve media</b>. Administrators (permission
<b>Edit reservations</b>) manage reservations from the search page.
@endtip

@section tutorial_reservations_item_settings Reservation settings per item

Whether items may be reserved and how long a reservation is valid is
set for the whole collection in the settings (see
@ref tutorial_settings_collection). Individual items can deviate from
this: users with the permission <b>Edit item settings</b> (by default
only the administrator) see the group <b>Reservation Settings</b> when
editing an item:

- <b>Allow reservations:</b> <b>Default (collection setting)</b>,
  <b>Allowed</b> or <b>Not allowed</b>. With <b>Not allowed</b> the
  item cannot be reserved.
- <b>Override period:</b> a separate validity in days for reservations
  of this item.

---

@page tutorial_return Returning

A return completes an existing lending and increases the available
stock.

@section tutorial_return_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>return item</b>.

@section tutorial_return_step1 Step 1: Selecting the Item

1. Enter the barcode, name or ISBN of the item being returned into
   the search field. The table only shows items that are currently
   lent.
2. Select the item and click <b>next</b>. When scanning a barcode,
   the item is selected automatically.

@image html 060_return_01.jpg "Return -- step 1: selecting the item"
@image latex 060_return_01.jpg "Return -- step 1: selecting the item"

@section tutorial_return_step2 Step 2: Ticking the Person(s)

1. The list only shows persons who currently have the selected item,
   together with their <b>class</b>. Clicking a column header sorts
   the list, e.g. by class. If a person holds several copies, their
   number is shown in the <b>Note</b> column.
2. Tick the returning person and click <b>next</b>.

If only one person has borrowed the item, that person is already
ticked and this step is skipped automatically.

@image html 061_return_02.jpg "Return -- step 2: ticking the person"
@image latex 061_return_02.jpg "Return -- step 2: ticking the person"

@subsection tutorial_return_group Several Persons at Once (Group Return)

When a group returns its copies -- e.g. a class its class set (see
@ref tutorial_group_lending) -- simply tick several persons in
step 2:

- <b>Class</b> -- restricts the list to one class.
- <b>Select all</b> / <b>Deselect all</b> -- affect the persons
  currently visible. If someone is missing, select all first and
  then untick the missing persons.
- <b>name/barcode</b> -- filters by name; a scanned <b>library
  card</b> ticks the person right away.

The bottom left shows how many persons are selected. With several
persons ticked, Colman takes back <b>all copies still on loan</b> to
them: step 3 is skipped, in step 4 you only enter remarks, and the
summary lists all persons and the total number of copies. Everything
is stored at once -- either for all persons or for none. Partial
returns or extending the due date are only possible for a single
person.

@image html 065_return_06.jpg "Group return -- step 2: ticking several persons"
@image latex 065_return_06.jpg "Group return -- step 2: ticking several persons"

@image html 066_return_07.jpg "Group return -- summary"
@image latex 066_return_07.jpg "Group return -- summary"

@section tutorial_return_step3 Step 3: Selecting the Lending

If the person has borrowed the item several times (e.g. at different
times), the table shows all open lendings. Select the matching one.

If there is only one open lending, this step is skipped
automatically.

@image html 062_return_03.jpg "Return -- step 3: selecting the lending"
@image latex 062_return_03.jpg "Return -- step 3: selecting the lending"

@section tutorial_return_step4 Step 4: Return Details

Shown are: person, item, number lent, number already returned and
number still lent.

Check or change:

- <b>number</b> -- how many copies are returned (range: 1 to the
  number still lent).
- <b>remarks</b> -- free text field.

Colman automatically records the current date and time as the time
of return. Click <b>next</b>.

@tip
If the person only brings the item by to keep it longer, click
<b>extend due date...</b> instead. The dialog only asks for the new
due date of exactly this lending (see @ref tutorial_lending_extend).
After extending, you can cancel the return with <b>back</b>.
@endtip

@image html 063_return_04.jpg "Return -- step 4: details"
@image latex 063_return_04.jpg "Return -- step 4: details"

@section tutorial_return_step5 Step 5: Summary and Completion

A summary shows person, item, number, date and remarks. Click
<b>finish</b>.

Then the completion page follows with the question <b>Further
returned items?</b>:

- <b>yes</b> -- take back another item.
- <b>no</b> -- back to the central navigation page.

@image html 064_return_05.jpg "Return -- step 5: summary"
@image latex 064_return_05.jpg "Return -- step 5: summary"

---

@page tutorial_search Search

The search page offers a targeted full-text search across all items
of the collection. For each hit, a detail view with the active
lendings and reservations can be opened.

@section tutorial_search_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>Search</b>.

@section tutorial_search_layout Page Layout

The search page has two columns:

<b>Left column -- search fields</b>

- <b>Type</b> -- restricts the search to one media type; <b>All
  types</b> searches the whole collection.
- <b>Search (all fields)</b> -- searches all text fields at the same
  time. Several search terms are separated by spaces and combined
  with AND.
- <b>Name/Title</b> -- filters by the title of the item only.
- <b>Subject</b> -- filters by subject (for school items).
- <b>Description</b> -- filters by the description of the content.
- <b>Keywords</b> -- filters by keywords.
- <b>Grade level</b> -- two selection fields for the range of grades
  (<b>any</b> = no restriction).
- Below follow groups with the type-specific fields (e.g. publisher,
  publication date, director). If a type is selected, only its group
  is shown.

All search fields work together: the more fields are filled in, the
narrower the set of hits. The list of hits is updated while you
type.

<b>Right column -- list of hits</b>

The table shows all hits with Name/Title, Type, Available, Lent and
Reserved. Below the table, these buttons are available -- depending
on the permissions:

- <b>Print...</b> and <b>Export CSV...</b> -- see
  @ref tutorial_search_print.
- <b>Reserve</b> or <b>Cancel reservation</b> -- for the selected
  item (see @ref tutorial_reservations).
- <b>Lend</b> -- starts the @ref tutorial_lending "lending" with the
  selected item.
- <b>Edit item</b> -- see @ref tutorial_search_edit.
- <b>Delete item</b> -- removes an item of which no copies are left
  in stock (after confirmation).

@image html 110_search.jpg "Search page with list of hits"
@image latex 110_search.jpg "Search page with list of hits"

<b>Detail view</b>

A <b>double click</b> on a hit opens the detail view of the item
with:

- the cover image (with <b>zoom</b>),
- the <b>Item Information</b>: type, Colman barcode, place,
  description, for school items subject and grade level, and the
  type-specific attributes (e.g. author, publisher, publication
  date),
- the <b>Active Lendings</b> (borrower, number lent and returned),
- the <b>Active Reservations</b>, if any.

@image html 111_search_detail.jpg "Detail view of an item"
@image latex 111_search_detail.jpg "Detail view of an item"

With the button <b>print label</b> at the top right of the detail
view, you can reprint the book label of the item at any time -- for
example to replace a damaged label. Colman opens the label printing
page with the item already checked; there you choose the layout and,
if needed, the number of copies. <b>back</b> then takes you back to
the detail view and from there to the list of hits.

@section tutorial_search_load_more Many Hits: Load More

For large collections, Colman initially loads only part of the
search results. Below the table, the number of currently loaded
entries is shown (e.g. <em>150 item(s) shown</em>). If the button
<b>Load more...</b> appears, there are further hits that are not yet
visible. A click appends the next set of hits to the list without
discarding the previous results.

If all search fields are empty and the collection is very large, the
list stays empty until at least one search term has been entered.

@tip
Enter one or more search terms to narrow down the set of hits and
find the desired item faster.
@endtip

@section tutorial_search_edit Editing an Item

Items can be edited directly from the search page:

1. Select the item in the list of hits.
2. Click <b>Edit item</b>.
3. Adjust the desired fields on the editing page.
4. Confirm with <b>Save</b>. The list of hits is then updated.

@tip The button <b>Edit item</b> is only visible to users with the
permission <b>Record acquisitions</b>.
@endtip

@section tutorial_search_print Printing and CSV Export

Two buttons are available below the table of results.

<b>Step 1 -- Making a selection (optional)</b>

<b>Ctrl+click</b> or <b>Shift+click</b> selects several rows.
Without a selection, all hits are used for the export.

<b>Step 2 -- Opening the selection page</b>

Click <b>Print...</b> or <b>Export CSV...</b>. On the page you
choose:

- <em>All items</em> or <em>Selected items</em>.
- Which fields appear in the list (all preselected).
- When printing: whether cover images are included
  (<b>Include thumbnails</b>).

<b>Step 3a -- Printing</b>

Click <b>Print...</b> on the page. The print preview opens, from
which you start printing.

<b>Step 3b -- Exporting CSV</b>

Click <b>Export CSV...</b> and choose a location. The file created
has MARC21 column headers (e.g. <tt>245$a</tt>, <tt>100$a</tt>) and
can be imported into Koha directly.

@tip
The CSV export is also suitable for importing into LibreOffice Calc
to create or process inventory lists.
@endtip

---

@page tutorial_open_lendings Open Lendings

The page <b>Open Lendings</b> lists all lendings that have not yet
been fully returned and allows printing them -- e.g. for reminders or
as a daily overview at the lending desk.

@section tutorial_open_lendings_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>open lendings</b>.

@section tutorial_open_lendings_layout Page Layout

The table shows all open lendings with due date, borrower, class,
count and item name. Two filter fields restrict the view:

- <b>Person</b> -- shows only the lendings of a particular person.
- <b>Class</b> -- shows only the lendings of a particular class (if
  recorded).

The button <b>Print</b> opens a print preview of the filtered list.
If <b>Number entries</b> is checked, every row of the printout gets a
consecutive number.

@image html 120_open_lendings.jpg "Open lendings"
@image latex 120_open_lendings.jpg "Open lendings"

@image html 121_open_lendings_preview.jpg "Open lendings: print preview"
@image latex 121_open_lendings_preview.jpg "Open lendings: print preview"

@tip
Use the person filter to quickly check all open lendings of a pupil
at the end of the school year.
@endtip

@section tutorial_open_lendings_extend Extending Due Dates

1. If needed, narrow the list with <b>Person</b> or <b>Class</b>.
2. Select the lendings whose due date is to be extended (Ctrl-click
   for single rows, Shift-click for a range, Ctrl+A for all shown).
3. Click <b>extend due date...</b> (bottom left).
4. The dialog shows the selected lendings for checking. Check the
   <b>new due date</b> (suggestion: latest current due date plus the
   default lending period or 14 days, see
   @ref tutorial_lending_extend) and click <b>Extend</b>.

The table then shows the new due dates; the extended lendings remain
selected. A double click on a row opens the return as before.

@image html 122_open_lendings_extend.jpg "Open lendings: extending due dates"
@image latex 122_open_lendings_extend.jpg "Open lendings: extending due dates"

@tip
Before the holidays, you can extend all lendings of a class to the
end of the holidays in one step this way.
@endtip

---

@page tutorial_history History (Transaction History)

The history shows all recorded transactions in one common,
chronologically sorted table.

@section tutorial_history_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>view history</b>.

@section tutorial_history_table Table

The table contains all actions (acquisitions, liquidations,
lendings, returns) in a single view with the columns date/time,
action, name of the item, number, user and -- for the administrator
-- data collector.

The fields <b>From</b> and <b>To</b> restrict the period;
<b>Apply</b> reloads the table for this period. If <b>show all</b>
is checked, all actions are shown regardless of the date. The
selection list to the right restricts the table to one type of
action, e.g. only lendings; <b>all actions</b> removes the
restriction again. The table can be sorted by any column (click on
the column header).

Below the table, a label shows the number of currently loaded
actions (e.g. <em>350 action(s) shown</em>). With very many entries,
<b>Load more...</b> appears -- a click appends further actions to
the table without discarding entries already loaded.

@image html 100_history.jpg "History overview"
@image latex 100_history.jpg "History overview"

<b>show details</b> (or a double click on a row) opens a window with
all details of the selected action.

@par Deleting actions
With the permission <b>Change and delete actions</b>, entries can be
permanently removed from the history. To do so, select the entry and click <b>delete</b>. For
actions that depend on each other (e.g. a return belonging to a
lending), Colman shows all affected entries for confirmation before
they are deleted together.

With Ctrl-click or Shift-click you mark several entries or a whole
range, e.g. all lendings of a mistaken group lending; <b>delete</b>
then removes all marked entries together with their dependent actions
in one step. <b>show details</b> and <b>change</b> only appear when
exactly one entry is marked.

@image html 101_history_delete.jpg "History: deleting entries"
@image latex 101_history_delete.jpg "History: deleting entries"

@warning Deleting history entries cannot be undone.
@endwarning

@par Changing actions
With the same permission, individual history entries can be
corrected later --
e.g. a wrongly entered date. Select the entry and click
<b>change</b>. A dialog shows the editable fields of the entry.
Changes that would violate the logical sequence (e.g. a return date
before the lending date) are rejected by Colman with an explanation
of the conflicts.

@image html 102_history_change.jpg "History: changing an entry"
@image latex 102_history_change.jpg "History: changing an entry"

The button <b>back</b> takes you back to the central navigation
page.

---

@page tutorial_settings Settings

The settings page is divided into four tabs. Which tabs you see
depends on your permissions:

- <b>Collection Settings</b> – permission <b>Edit preferences</b>
- <b>Program Settings</b> – permission <b>Edit program settings</b>
- <b>Accessibility</b> – everybody
- <b>Encryption</b> – administrator only

@section tutorial_settings_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>Settings</b>.

@section tutorial_settings_collection Tab: Collection Settings

Here the basic data of the collection, defined when it was created,
is managed:

- <b>collection name</b> -- shown on library cards and barcodes
- <b>organization</b> -- school or institution
- <b>responsible</b> -- contact person
- <b>logo</b> -- image file shown on library cards; chosen with
  <b>browse</b>
- <b>default lending period</b> -- default lending period in years,
  months and days
- <b>Allow reservations</b> -- whether items may be reserved and for
  how many days a reservation is valid
- <b>Default type for new items</b> -- media type preselected when
  entering items
- <b>items are school items by default</b> -- new items
  automatically get the school fields
- <b>Signature top categories</b> -- list of suggestions for the
  upper signature (e.g. subject areas); <b>add</b> and <b>remove</b>
  maintain the list
- <b>Signature bottom categories (for selected top)</b> --
  suggestions for the lower signature, each for the top category
  selected above

@image html 130_settings_01.jpg "Settings: collection settings"
@image latex 130_settings_01.jpg "Settings: collection settings"

@section tutorial_settings_program Tab: Program Settings

These settings apply to all collections.

<b>Thumbnail directory</b>

The local directory in which cover images of items are stored;
chosen with <b>browse</b>.

<b>ISBN look-up</b>

Colman can automatically search the internet for book data when an
item is entered. To protect your privacy, Colman only connects to
the services you explicitly enable here:

- <b>search ISBN on openlibrary.org</b> -- free service, no API key
  needed
- <b>search ISBN on www.googleapis.com</b> -- Google Books;
  optionally with your own <b>Google Books API key</b> (further down
  on the tab)

In addition, Colman queries the active SRU library catalogues (see
below). Enable at least one service to use the ISBN look-up.

<b>SRU servers (library catalogues)</b>

The table lists public library catalogues that Colman queries via
SRU -- preconfigured are, among others, the German National Library
(DNB) and K10plus. Each row can be switched on or off with the check
box in the column <b>Enable</b>. The columns <b>ISBN field</b>,
<b>EAN field</b> and <b>Title field</b> contain the search indexes
of the respective catalogue:

- <b>ISBN field</b> / <b>EAN field</b> -- for the barcode search
- <b>Title field</b> -- for the
  @ref tutorial_items_title_search "internet title search"; if the
  field is empty, the server is skipped in the title search

<b>Services for the title search</b>

Besides the SRU servers, the title search and the EAN search use the
following services:

- <b>search product barcode on MusicBrainz</b> -- CDs; free service,
  no API key needed
- <b>search board games on boardgamegeek.com</b> -- requires a
  <b>boardgamegeek.com API token</b>
- <b>look up EAN on opengtindb.org</b> -- games, DVDs, Blu-rays and
  more; requires an <b>opengtindb.org user ID</b>

Further down on the tab follow:

- <b>proxy user name</b> and <b>proxy password</b> -- only needed if
  internet access goes through a proxy requiring authentication
- <b>try to download thumbnails</b> -- download cover images
  automatically during the internet look-up
- <b>internet lookup timeout</b> -- maximum waiting time for a query
  in seconds
- <b>Search threshold</b> -- how many items lists load initially
  before <b>Load more...</b> appears (0 = always all)

@tip
For school libraries in German-speaking countries, the preconfigured
catalogues DNB and K10plus are usually sufficient; elsewhere, add the
SRU server of your national library. The DNB also catalogues DVDs and
Blu-rays as specific releases -- with distributor, running time,
language versions and age rating.
@endtip

<b>Barcode bar width</b>

The width of the narrowest bar (X dimension) of the printed Code 128
barcodes. The default value of 0.33&nbsp;mm is the value recommended
for reliable scanning.

@tip
Only reduce this value if the barcodes must fit on very small
labels -- and test beforehand whether your scanner still reads the
finer bars reliably.
@endtip

@image html 131_settings_02.jpg "Settings: program settings"
@image latex 131_settings_02.jpg "Settings: program settings"


@section tutorial_accessibility Tab: Accessibility

Here the <b>Font size</b> of the whole application can be adjusted.
Set the desired value in the number field -- a preview shows the
effect immediately.

Alternatively, the font size can be changed at any time – even before
logging in:

- on the start page with the buttons <b>A+</b> and <b>A-</b>,
- with the keyboard shortcuts <b>Ctrl++</b> and <b>Ctrl+-</b>;
  <b>Ctrl+0</b> resets it to 12 points,
- with <b>Ctrl + mouse wheel</b>.

The allowed range is 8 to 24 points. The setting applies to the whole
program and is kept for the next start.

@image html 132_settings_03.jpg "Settings: accessibility"
@image latex 132_settings_03.jpg "Settings: accessibility"

@section tutorial_encryption Tab: Encryption

Encryption is <b>optional</b> and disabled by default. If your
collection contains personal data (names of pupils, lending
history), it is advisable to encrypt the `.col` file so that
unauthorized persons cannot read any data when accessing the file
directly.

Colman encrypts the entire database file with its own password. The
current status (<b>encrypted</b> or <b>not encrypted</b>) is shown at
the top of the tab as <b>Encryption status</b>. If the collection is
encrypted, the page for entering the database password appears when
the file is opened (see @ref tutorial_open_encrypted).

<b>Enable Encryption</b>
(only visible if the collection is not yet encrypted)

1. Click <b>Enable Encryption</b>.
2. Enter a password and confirm it. A strength indicator shows the
   quality of the password.
3. Click <b>OK</b>. From now on, the password is requested whenever
   the file is opened.

<b>Change Encryption Password</b>
(only visible if the collection is already encrypted)

1. Click <b>Change Password</b>.
2. Enter the current and the new password.
3. Click <b>OK</b>.

<b>Disable Encryption</b>
(only visible if the collection is already encrypted)

1. Click <b>Disable Encryption</b>.
2. Confirm with the current password.

@image html 133_settings_04.jpg "Settings: encryption"
@image latex 133_settings_04.jpg "Settings: encryption"

@warning The password for the file encryption is independent of the
administrator password. If you forget the file password, the `.col`
file can no longer be opened -- not even by Colman itself.
@endwarning

@section tutorial_settings_save Saving Changes

- <b>apply</b> -- saves the changes of the active tab.
- <b>revert</b> -- discards the changes and restores the saved
  values.
- <b>back</b> -- leaves the page. If there are unsaved changes, you
  are asked whether you want to apply them.

---

@page tutorial_prefs Program Settings (on the Settings Page)

The program settings -- ISBN look-up, thumbnail directory and more --
are described under
@ref tutorial_settings_program "Settings → Program Settings".

---

@page tutorial_printer_calibration Calibrating the Printer (Administrator Only)

If printed labels or library cards appear slightly shifted or
distorted, the printer calibration corrects this. Colman stores a
separate calibration for each printer, which is applied
automatically to the next print job.

@section tutorial_printer_calibration_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>calibrate printer</b>.

@image html 020_printer_calibration.jpg "Calibrating the printer"
@image latex 020_printer_calibration.jpg "Calibrating the printer"

@section tutorial_printer_calibration_steps Calibration Procedure

First choose the printer to be calibrated in the field
<b>Printer</b> at the top. If calibration values are already stored
for it, they are shown in step&nbsp;3.

<b>Step 1 -- Print calibration sheet</b>

1. Put a sheet of plain paper into the printer.
2. Click <b>Print calibration sheet</b> and confirm the print dialog
   with the default settings (do not set any page margins or
   scaling). The sheet contains three crosshairs at fixed positions:
   - <b>A</b> at (50&nbsp;mm | 50&nbsp;mm) from the paper edge
   - <b>B</b> at (150&nbsp;mm | 50&nbsp;mm) -- 100&nbsp;mm to the
     right of A
   - <b>C</b> at (50&nbsp;mm | 150&nbsp;mm) -- 100&nbsp;mm below A

The preview on the right shows the calibration sheet.

<b>Step 2 -- Measure and compute offset</b>

1. Measure the actual distances of the crosshair centres from the
   left and top paper edge with a ruler and enter the four values:
   - <b>Cross A -- X distance</b> -- horizontal distance of cross A
     from the left edge
   - <b>Cross A -- Y distance</b> -- vertical distance of cross A
     from the top edge
   - <b>Cross B -- X distance</b> -- horizontal distance of cross B
     from the left edge
   - <b>Cross C -- Y distance</b> -- vertical distance of cross C
     from the top edge
2. Click <b>Compute offset</b>. Colman calculates the necessary
   correction values for offset and scaling from them.

<b>Step 3 -- Review and save calibration</b>

This area shows the calculated correction values:

- <b>X offset</b> -- horizontal shift in mm
- <b>Y offset</b> -- vertical shift in mm
- <b>X scale</b> -- horizontal scaling factor (1.0 = no correction)
- <b>Y scale</b> -- vertical scaling factor (1.0 = no correction)

- Click <b>Save</b> to store the values permanently for this
  printer.
- Click <b>Reset</b> to restore the last saved values.
- Click <b>back</b> to return to the central navigation page without
  saving.

@note The calibration is stored per printer. If you use the same
printer under different operating system names (e.g. after a printer
driver update), you have to repeat the calibration.

@section tutorial_printer_calibration_effect Effect of the Calibration

After saving, the calibration is applied automatically to all print
jobs on this printer -- both when printing labels
(@ref tutorial_item_barcodes) and library cards
(@ref tutorial_person_barcodes). A new calibration is only needed if
the printer is replaced or the printer driver is reinstalled.

---

@page tutorial_passwords Managing Credentials

@section tutorial_passwords_admin Credential Management (Administrator)

The administrator can set or delete user names and passwords for all
persons.

1. On the central navigation page, click <b>Change Passwords</b>.
2. On the left, a table with all persons (last name, first name,
   title, user name) and the administrator appears.
3. Select the desired person.

<b>Managing user names:</b>

4. Enter a unique name into the field <b>Username</b>.
5. Click <b>Set Username</b>. If the name is already taken, an error
   message appears.
6. To remove a user name, click <b>Remove Username</b> (confirmation
   required).

<b>Managing passwords:</b>

7. Enter the password into the fields <b>New password</b> and
   <b>Confirm</b>.
8. Click <b>Change Password</b>.
9. To delete a password (e.g. for pupils who have left), click
   <b>Delete Password</b>.

@image html 150_passwords.jpg "Credential management (administrator)"
@image latex 150_passwords.jpg "Credential management (administrator)"

@section tutorial_passwords_user Changing the Password (User)

Regular users can change their own password:

1. On the central navigation page, click <b>Change Password</b>.
2. Enter your <b>Current password</b>.
3. Enter your <b>New password</b> and the <b>Confirm</b>ation.
4. Click <b>OK</b>.

@note Passwords are hashed with the Argon2 algorithm.

---

@page tutorial_backup Automatic Backup

Colman automatically creates backup copies of the `.col` file in the
same directory. The backup files get a consecutive number as suffix,
e.g.:

    my_collection.col.~1~
    my_collection.col.~2~
    my_collection.col.~3~

A backup is created on the first save of a session and then
automatically after every 16 changes. As soon as there are more than
20 backup files, Colman asks whether older ones should be deleted
(the 10 newest are always kept).

In case of an error, rename the backup file with the highest number
to the original file name (remove the suffix) to restore the last
saved state.

---

@page tutorial_types Managing Media Types

Media types (e.g. "Book", "DVD", "Periodical") define which fields
an item has. Managing media types requires the permission <b>Edit
categories</b>. When a new database is created, six types are already
predefined; your own types can be added at any time.

@section tutorial_types_navigate Navigation

On the central navigation page, click <b>manage categories</b>.

@section tutorial_types_overview Page Layout

Left: list of the existing media types (<b>known item types</b>).
Right: <b>item type name</b>, <b>search category</b> and the table
<b>item type fields</b> of the selected type.

The display names of the predefined types and fields exist in English
and German; Colman shows them in the language of the user interface.

The <b>search category</b> defines which internet services are
queried when an item of this type is entered (book, CD, DVD, Blu-ray,
board game or <b>none</b>). It is independent of the displayed name
of the type.

Below the table, <b>add field</b>, <b>remove field</b>, <b>remove
all</b>, <b>Move up</b> and <b>Move down</b> arrange the fields; the
buttons below them (<b>insert type</b>, <b>change type</b>,
<b>remove type</b>, <b>revert</b>) save or discard the changes.

@image html 140_item_types.jpg "Media type management"
@image latex 140_item_types.jpg "Media type management"

@section tutorial_types_predefined Predefined Media Types

Every newly created database automatically contains six media types:

- <b>Book</b>: Authors, Edition, Publication date, Publisher, Pages,
  ISBN-10, ISBN-13, DDC
- <b>Periodical</b>: ISSN, Publisher, Year of publication, Extent,
  Note
- <b>CD</b>: Artist, Publisher number, Publisher, Year of
  publication, Language, Extent, Content type, Media type, Carrier
  type, EAN
- <b>DVD</b>: Director/cast, Publisher, Year of publication,
  Language, Extent, Content type, Media type, Carrier type,
  Participants, Target audience, EAN
- <b>Blu-ray</b>: (same as DVD)
- <b>Board game</b>: Designer, Illustrator, Publisher, Year of
  publication, Min. players, Max. players, Minimum age, Playing time
  (min.), Language, Genre, EAN

These types can be changed or deleted like types you created
yourself.

@section tutorial_types_common Fixed Fields

In addition to the type-specific fields, every item, regardless of
its type, always has the following fixed fields:

- <b>Item name</b> (MARC21: 245$a) -- name or title of the item
- <b>Colman barcode</b> -- the automatically assigned barcode
- <b>Thumbnail</b> -- cover image
- <b>School item</b> -- marks the item as a school item (with
  subject, academic year and kind)
- <b>Place</b> -- shelf location
- <b>Signature top</b> / <b>Signature bottom</b> -- text of the spine
  label
- <b>Description</b> (MARC21: 520$a) -- short description or summary
- <b>Keywords</b> (MARC21: 650$a) -- topical keywords

These fields appear greyed out in the table <b>item type fields</b>
and cannot be removed, but they can be moved with <b>Move up</b> /
<b>Move down</b>. The order determines the arrangement in the item
form.

@section tutorial_types_add Adding a New Media Type

1. Enter the name into the field <b>item type name</b>.
2. Click <b>add field</b> to add type-specific attributes.
3. Define the data type for each field (see the list below).
4. Optional: assign a MARC21 tag to each field (see
   @ref tutorial_types_marc21).
5. Optional: use the MARC21 template to insert a complete set of
   fields at once (see @ref tutorial_types_template).
6. Change the order of the fields with <b>Move up</b> and <b>Move
   down</b>.
7. Click <b>insert type</b>.

Available field types:

- <b>short text</b> -- single-line text input (e.g. author,
  publisher)
- <b>long text</b> -- multi-line text input (e.g. summary)
- <b>integer</b> -- whole number, optionally with min/max (e.g.
  number of pages)
- <b>float</b> -- decimal number, optionally with min/max (e.g.
  price)
- <b>date</b> -- date field (e.g. publication date)
- <b>time</b> -- time field
- <b>duration</b> -- duration in the format HH:MM (e.g. running time
  of a film)
- <b>integer ranges</b> -- ranges of whole numbers (e.g. grades
  5-10)
- <b>table</b> -- nested table (e.g. list of copies)

@section tutorial_types_marc21 Assigning MARC21 Fields

The library format MARC21 defines standardized identifiers for
bibliographic fields (e.g. 245$a stands for the title, 100$a for the
main author). Colman can assign such a MARC21 tag to each
type-specific field.

The column <b>field name</b> of the table contains a selection list
with grouped MARC21 fields. Proceed as follows:

1. Click into the selection field of the column <b>field name</b>.
2. Choose the matching MARC21 field from the list, e.g. author
   (100$a) from the group main entry.
3. Colman automatically sets the data type to the recommended type.
4. Alternatively, type your own name; then no MARC21 tag is stored.

The MARC21 tag is not shown in the item form; it serves consistent
naming and a possible later data transfer.

@section tutorial_types_template Using a MARC21 Template

Instead of adding fields one by one, you can take over a ready-made
selection of fields for common media types:

1. Click the button <b>MARC21 template</b>.
2. Choose one of the templates from the menu and confirm.

Available templates:

- <b>Book</b>: author, ISBN, edition, publisher, publication year,
  pages, DDC, genre
- <b>CD / Audio</b>: artist, publisher number, publisher, publication
  year, language, extent, content type, media type, carrier type
- <b>DVD / Video</b>: director, publication year, language, extent,
  content type, media type, carrier type, participants, target
  audience
- <b>Periodical</b>: ISSN, publisher, publication year, extent, note

The template only inserts fields that are not yet covered by a fixed
field. "Title" and "Description" are therefore not inserted again,
since they already exist as fixed fields.

@note Existing type-specific fields are not deleted. The template
only adds new fields at the end of the list.

@section tutorial_types_repeatable Repeatable Fields

Some fields can occur several times for one item, for example when a
book has several authors. Such fields are marked as
<b>Repeatable</b>.

In the item form, repeatable fields have a <b>+</b> button. Click it
to add another entry. The <b>-</b> button removes an entry again.

For the predefined types, the following fields are repeatable:

- <b>Book</b>: Authors (100$a), ISBN-10 / ISBN-13 (020$a), DDC (082$a)
- <b>CD</b>: Artist (100$a)
- <b>DVD</b> and <b>Blu-ray</b>: Director/cast (700$a)
- <b>Board game</b>: Designer and Illustrator (700$a), Language
  (546$a), Genre (655$a)

When creating your own fields, enable repeatability with the check
box <b>Repeatable</b> in the field's row.

@section tutorial_types_change Changing a Media Type

1. Select the type in the list on the left.
2. Change the name or attributes.
3. Click <b>change type</b>.

@warning Data can be lost when changing a media type. Consider
creating a new type instead and making a backup copy beforehand.
@endwarning

@section tutorial_types_remove Deleting a Media Type

1. Select the type in the list.
2. Click <b>remove type</b>.
3. Confirm the security prompt.

@warning Deleting a type also removes all associated items,
acquisitions, liquidations, lendings and returns -- irreversibly!
@endwarning

---

@page tutorial_permissions Permissions overview

@tableofcontents

Each person has a set of permissions. They determine which tiles
appear on the central navigation page and what the person may do.
The administrator always has all permissions. How to change the
permissions of a person is described in
@ref tutorial_persons_permissions.

@section tutorial_permissions_list Available permissions

| Permission | Allows |
|---|---|
| Edit persons | Create, change and delete persons and grant them one's own permissions |
| Create borrowers | Create and edit borrowers only (view and reserve items) |
| Create data collectors | Create and edit only persons who record acquisitions or liquidations |
| Record lendings | Lend items |
| Record returns | Record returns |
| Record acquisitions | Acquire items |
| Record liquidations | Liquidate items |
| View history | View the history |
| View person lists | Person lists and filters, reminder of due returns |
| View item lists | The item search |
| Edit preferences | The tab Collection Settings |
| Print ID cards | Print ID cards |
| Print labels | Print item labels |
| Edit categories | Manage media types and signature categories |
| View personal data | View one's own data and lendings |
| Manage label layouts | Create and delete label sheet layouts |
| Calibrate printers | Edit the printer calibration |
| Edit ID card layout | Edit the ID card, item card and signature layouts |
| Print signature labels | Print signature (spine) labels |
| Change and delete actions | Change or delete entries in the history |
| Edit program settings | The tab Program Settings |
| Manage classes | Class management and the school year change |
| Reserve media | Reserve items for oneself (default: yes) |
| Edit reservations | Create, change and delete reservations for others (default: no) |
| Edit item settings | Reservation settings of individual items (default: no) |

@section tutorial_permissions_tiles Tiles of the central page

A tile only appears if the logged-in person has the matching
permission:

| Tile | Required permission |
|---|---|
| manage borrowers | Edit persons, Create borrowers or Create data collectors |
| acquire | Record acquisitions |
| lend item | Record lendings (persons who may only <b>Reserve media</b> see <b>reserve item</b> instead) |
| return item | Record returns |
| liquidate | Record liquidations |
| Search | View item lists |
| view history | View history |
| print IDs | Print ID cards |
| print labels | Print labels |
| print signatures | Print signature labels |
| edit ID layout, edit item card layout, edit signature layout | Edit ID card layout |
| manage categories | Edit categories |
| class management | Manage classes |
| My reservations | Reserve media |
| Settings, open lendings | visible to everybody; the contents depend on the permissions |
| calibrate printer | administrator only |

@section tutorial_permissions_rules Rules for granting permissions

- <b>You can only grant permissions you hold yourself.</b> Permissions
  you may not grant are greyed out. This prevents anybody from gaining
  additional rights through a second account.
- Persons who may only <b>Create borrowers</b> or <b>Create data
  collectors</b> can only edit persons whose permissions lie entirely
  within their own – i.e. no staff or administrator accounts.
- New persons may by default only <b>Reserve media</b> and <b>View
  personal data</b>.

---

@page tutorial_multi_user Multi-user operation: server and clients

@tableofcontents

If several workstations are to work with the same collection at the
same time, the database lives on a server machine (for example a
Raspberry Pi). The server `colman_rest_server` provides the collection
on the network; the workstations run `colman_rest_client`, which has
the same user interface as the single-user version. The Colman app for
Android connects to this server as well.

@section tutorial_multi_user_server Starting the server

When started for the first time with a database file that does not
exist yet, the server creates the collection and asks for the
administrator password:

@code
colman_rest_server --db /srv/colman/school.col --port 8080
@endcode

On all later starts the same command opens the existing collection.
An encrypted collection additionally needs the database password
(`--key-file` or the environment variable `COLMAN_DB_KEY`).

@warning Outside a trusted local network the server must use TLS
(`--cert` and `--key`); otherwise passwords are transmitted
unencrypted.
@endwarning

@section tutorial_multi_user_client Logging in at a workstation

Start `colman_rest_client`. The login page automatically offers
servers found on the local network; otherwise enter the server address
(e.g. `http://server:8080`) manually. Log in with your username and
password – the first time as `admin` with the password chosen when the
server was started. The server address is remembered for the next
start.

@section tutorial_multi_user_refresh Working at the same time

Each workstation regularly asks the server for changes made by other
workstations and refreshes its lists automatically. You can set the
interval in the program settings under <b>poll interval (REST
client)</b> (default: 10 seconds). If another workstation lends an item
in the meantime, the displayed available count decreases accordingly.

@tip For centrally managed user accounts the server can accept logins
via an OpenID Connect provider (e.g. Authentik). The setup and all
server options are described in the project README.
@endtip

---

*/

/**

@page tutorial_appendix_errors Appendix: Error Display

@tableofcontents

When Colman encounters an error -- for example when connecting to
the server -- the program shows a comprehensible error message. In
addition, a button <b>Details...</b> appears, which gives access to
technical information.

@section tutorial_appendix_errors_message Error Message

If an action fails, a red text with a short explanation appears.
Typical messages are:

- <b>Wrong username or password.</b> -- the login data is not
  correct.
- <b>Cannot connect to the server. Please check the server URL.</b>
  -- the server cannot be reached or the address entered is wrong.
- <b>Server error (status …).</b> -- the server returned an
  unexpected error code.

<!-- @image html 200_error_display.jpg "Error message with details button" -->
<!-- @image latex 200_error_display.jpg "Error message with details button" -->

@section tutorial_appendix_errors_details Showing Technical Details

Click <b>Details...</b> to open a dialog with the exact technical
description of the error. The window has the title <b>Error
Details</b> and shows:

- the HTTP status code (e.g. <b>HTTP 401</b>), and
- the error message returned by the server, if any (e.g. a JSON
  description).

<!-- @image html 201_error_details.jpg "Details dialog with HTTP status code" -->
<!-- @image latex 201_error_details.jpg "Details dialog with HTTP status code" -->

@section tutorial_appendix_errors_copy Copying the Error Message

The error text shown can be selected and copied to the clipboard.
This way you can pass the message on to the administrator or paste
it into an error report:

1. Click into the error text in the details window.
2. Select the desired text (e.g. with the mouse or with
   <b>Ctrl+A</b> for everything).
3. Copy it to the clipboard with <b>Ctrl+C</b>.

@tip
When reporting an error, please always copy the complete content of
the details window. This makes the analysis much easier.
@endtip

---

*/
